WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.043
- You got two
seats at Portillo's.

00:00:02.043 --> 00:00:04.587
Michelle Obama,
Jordan and Oprah:

00:00:04.587 --> 00:00:05.755
Who're you eating with?

00:00:05.755 --> 00:00:07.674
- Ahh, I'm dropping Jordan, man.

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:08.466
- [Channing Frye:] Ooh!

00:00:08.466 --> 00:00:09.634
- [Candace Parker:] No Portillo's for Jordan.

00:00:09.634 --> 00:00:10.301
- [Frye:] Ow.

00:00:10.301 --> 00:00:21.479
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:23.898
 
We're here at L'Angolo
Estate, where I found out

00:00:23.898 --> 00:00:25.567
that wine wasn't just
something I wanted

00:00:25.567 --> 00:00:27.277
to share with my
family but something I

00:00:27.277 --> 00:00:28.695
wanted to share with the world.

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:31.197
It's here that we created
Chosen Family Wines.

00:00:34.034 --> 00:00:37.787
 
 
Well, guys, we have an amazing,
amazing day in store for you,

00:00:37.787 --> 00:00:41.124
with some drinking, some wine,
talking hoops with the WNBA

00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:43.835
champion, a two-time
NCAA champion,

00:00:43.835 --> 00:00:46.546
Defensive Player of the
Year, five-time All-Star,

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:48.673
five-time Russian
League Champion,

00:00:48.673 --> 00:00:51.968
four-time Russian Cup,
one-time EuroLeague Champion.

00:00:51.968 --> 00:00:54.971
And, I mean, if we could look
at all these accolades,

00:00:54.971 --> 00:00:56.681
we would have to
do this all day.

00:00:56.681 --> 00:00:59.517
Well, I just want to say
thanks to Candace Nicole Parker

00:00:59.517 --> 00:01:01.478
for being here on "Full Bodied."

00:01:01.478 --> 00:01:03.271
As the wines get
darker, the questions

00:01:03.271 --> 00:01:04.272
are gonna get deeper.

00:01:04.272 --> 00:01:06.399
First of all, we have to
pop open this champagne.

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:09.069
I knew you were good.

00:01:09.069 --> 00:01:12.363
I did not know you
were on God-level good.

00:01:12.363 --> 00:01:14.407
Let's cheers to
you and everything

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:15.492
that you've accomplished.

00:01:15.492 --> 00:01:17.702
What I've seen over
the last few years,

00:01:17.702 --> 00:01:19.287
not only from what
you have done,

00:01:19.287 --> 00:01:21.456
from what a lot of
other powerful women

00:01:21.456 --> 00:01:23.291
have done in the NBA
and around sports,

00:01:23.291 --> 00:01:24.959
is change the
culture of how we

00:01:24.959 --> 00:01:27.587
see women in the
workplace, how we see women

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:30.423
as mothers and as professionals.

00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:32.383
And overall, you're
just cool as shit.
- [Laughing]

00:01:32.383 --> 00:01:35.261
- [Frye:] So, you know, cheers to you.

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:38.890
I think the first thing that
I want to talk about is 2016.

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:42.018
- [Commentator:] The LA Sparks have
slain the mighty Lynx.

00:01:42.018 --> 00:01:44.479
- [Commentator:] Candace Parker!

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:45.688
- 2016.

00:01:45.688 --> 00:01:51.361
Man, it's interesting that
you're talking about champagne

00:01:51.361 --> 00:01:54.948
at this point in time,
because I remember

00:01:54.948 --> 00:02:00.620
I got some Dom Perignon in 2008
- Ooh.
- that was given to me after we

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.455
won the NCAA championship.

00:02:02.455 --> 00:02:04.165
And they handed it
to me, and I decided

00:02:04.165 --> 00:02:07.585
I wasn't going to open
it until we won a WNBA

00:02:07.585 --> 00:02:10.046
championship.
- OK.
- You know, in 2008.

00:02:10.046 --> 00:02:11.214
So this was back in 2008.

00:02:11.214 --> 00:02:16.094
I'm thinking, like, yo, you know, maybe
one, maybe two years,

00:02:16.094 --> 00:02:18.429
maybe three, maybe four.
- [Laughing]

00:02:18.429 --> 00:02:21.516
- Ended up being a
whole lot longer
- [Laughing]

00:02:21.516 --> 00:02:23.768
- than one, maybe two years.

00:02:23.768 --> 00:02:26.354
- Here's something
that I didn't know.

00:02:26.354 --> 00:02:30.066
You played the end of your
rookie season pregnant.

00:02:30.066 --> 00:02:35.363
How in God's green Earth did
you play at an elite level

00:02:35.363 --> 00:02:37.115
not only taking
care of yourself

00:02:37.115 --> 00:02:40.410
but obviously taking care
of your healthy, amazing,

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.036
wonderful daughter
at the same time?

00:02:42.036 --> 00:02:46.833
- Honestly, I thought
I was just tired.

00:02:46.833 --> 00:02:48.251
You know, I had played a year straight.

00:02:48.251 --> 00:02:51.212
I had played ever since the year
before, with USA basketball,

00:02:51.212 --> 00:02:53.506
and then played all
of college and then

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:55.425
went straight to WNBA season.

00:02:55.425 --> 00:02:57.552
And then, from WNBA season,
I went to the Olympics,

00:02:57.552 --> 00:02:59.053
and then back to WNBA.

00:02:59.053 --> 00:03:01.472
And after the
Olympics, which is when

00:03:01.472 --> 00:03:04.058
I was pregnant with my daughter,
I just thought I was tired.

00:03:04.058 --> 00:03:05.560
So we pushed
through that season,

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.980
and, you know, it didn't end the way that
we wanted it to on the court,

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.191
in terms of we lost in
the Western Conference Final

00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:13.985
on a last-second shot, but
that's for another drink.

00:03:13.985 --> 00:03:14.944
- [Laughing] Listen, we got sips.

00:03:14.944 --> 00:03:18.156
- At the same time, you know, winning
Rookie of the Year and MVP,

00:03:18.156 --> 00:03:20.241
I wasn't really
thinking about that.
- Right.

00:03:20.241 --> 00:03:21.993
- It was, I was disappointed at
the end of the year,

00:03:21.993 --> 00:03:23.953
and then I got this
amazing surprise.

00:03:23.953 --> 00:03:24.913
Anybody that knows me,

00:03:24.913 --> 00:03:26.372
I've always wanted to be a mom.

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:28.958
And, honestly, it couldn't
have come at a better time.

00:03:28.958 --> 00:03:31.169
It was at the early
stage of my career where

00:03:31.169 --> 00:03:33.671
now my daughter and I
have grown up together.

00:03:33.671 --> 00:03:35.715
I don't remember
basketball before her.

00:03:35.715 --> 00:03:38.343
I mean, if you think about it, it
wasn't being pregnant playing.

00:03:38.343 --> 00:03:40.053
It was coming back
after pregnancy,

00:03:40.053 --> 00:03:41.971
to me, that was hard.
- Right.

00:03:41.971 --> 00:03:45.183
- She was six weeks old when I
came back and stepped up out

00:03:45.183 --> 00:03:46.976
on the court and played
for the first time.

00:03:46.976 --> 00:03:47.977
- My wife got pregnant.

00:03:47.977 --> 00:03:49.938
It took me six months to
get back to the court.

00:03:49.938 --> 00:03:51.648
[Both laughing]

00:03:51.648 --> 00:03:53.942
I think that needs
to get talked more.

00:03:53.942 --> 00:03:57.654
About how powerful that is,
that you guys are not just

00:03:57.654 --> 00:03:58.696
sitting on the side.

00:03:58.696 --> 00:04:00.323
And not that that's
wrong, but you guys

00:04:00.323 --> 00:04:02.700
are still continuing
to play the sport that

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:06.246
is so hard, that is so
physical, that is so daunting,

00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:09.791
at an elite level
and also growing

00:04:09.791 --> 00:04:11.417
another amazing human
being inside you.

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:12.001
- Cheers.

00:04:12.001 --> 00:04:14.462
Thank you, Channing.

00:04:14.462 --> 00:04:17.382
To completely throw this off,
since I've given you 10 minutes

00:04:17.382 --> 00:04:20.551
of compliments, you've got
two seats at Portillo's.

00:04:20.551 --> 00:04:23.471
Michelle Obama,
Jordan and Oprah:

00:04:23.471 --> 00:04:24.681
Who're you eating with?

00:04:24.681 --> 00:04:25.181
- Ahh,

00:04:25.181 --> 00:04:26.766
I'm dropping Jordan, man.

00:04:26.766 --> 00:04:27.809
I'm dropping Jordan.

00:04:27.809 --> 00:04:28.476
- [Frye:] Ooh!

00:04:28.476 --> 00:04:29.602
- No Portillo's for Jordan.

00:04:29.602 --> 00:04:30.270
- Ow.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.730
[Both laughing]

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:35.233
I think, honestly,
Jordan and Michelle Obama

00:04:35.233 --> 00:04:37.735
would both give up their seat
if I picked either one of them

00:04:37.735 --> 00:04:38.403
over Oprah.

00:04:38.403 --> 00:04:40.863
So I think that's it.
- [Laughing]

00:04:40.863 --> 00:04:42.240
On this next wine.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:45.702
This was new to me in
the last few years.
- OK.

00:04:45.702 --> 00:04:46.869
So it's a Chablis.

00:04:46.869 --> 00:04:51.332
What I know from white wine is
the old-school, big, buttery,

00:04:51.332 --> 00:04:52.417
oaky.

00:04:52.417 --> 00:04:55.420
You just take wet,
buttered popcorn.
- Mmhmm.
- Right?

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:57.422
That's what my
parents grew up on.

00:04:57.422 --> 00:04:59.674
That was my image of chardonnay.

00:04:59.674 --> 00:05:02.260
But we picked this for you,
because Chablis was made

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:03.636
from the exact same grape,

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:05.096
100 percent chardonnay.

00:05:05.096 --> 00:05:08.057
But instead of months in
oak, it's made in steel vats.

00:05:08.057 --> 00:05:09.934
It's in a different
environment but still has

00:05:09.934 --> 00:05:11.811
a legendary, classic taste.

00:05:11.811 --> 00:05:15.189
On the same note, without you
leaving L.A. to go to Chicago,

00:05:15.189 --> 00:05:17.233
we would never get to
witness your next step,

00:05:17.233 --> 00:05:19.319
the evolution, this Chablis.

00:05:19.319 --> 00:05:20.945
So I wanted to
share this with you

00:05:20.945 --> 00:05:23.614
and let you know how
excited I am of your future.

00:05:23.614 --> 00:05:24.282
- Thank you.

00:05:24.282 --> 00:05:25.575
I'm gonna open mine, though.

00:05:25.575 --> 00:05:27.452
- So, as you're
opening that, I think

00:05:27.452 --> 00:05:30.204
I've been around
more Chicago people

00:05:30.204 --> 00:05:32.790
than any other
city in the world,

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:36.836
because I played for Zeke,
played with Quentin Richardson,

00:05:36.836 --> 00:05:39.005
played with Eddy Curry.

00:05:39.005 --> 00:05:42.383
But what is it like
to be from Chicago?

00:05:42.383 --> 00:05:44.427
- Well, first, I wanna,
I wanna say, because I've

00:05:44.427 --> 00:05:49.140
gotten a lot of flak lately,
- [Laughing]
- where I don't claim to be from Chicago.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:50.933
I claim to be from Chicagoland.

00:05:50.933 --> 00:05:53.102
And I make this very clear.

00:05:53.102 --> 00:05:53.770
- Ooh, that's good.

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:55.730
- That I am from the suburbs.

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:56.898
Proud to be from Naperville.

00:05:56.898 --> 00:05:58.232
- [Frye:] Yeah, suburban commando.

00:05:58.232 --> 00:06:00.610
- Graduated high school
from Naperville Central.

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:03.571
But I graduated on the court
from the Chicagoland area.
- Yeah.

00:06:03.571 --> 00:06:07.241
- I played near, far,
downtown, western suburbs.

00:06:07.241 --> 00:06:09.494
You talk about north,
south, everywhere

00:06:09.494 --> 00:06:10.870
in the State of Illinois.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:14.248
You know, it's just a different
breed of people.
- Mmm.

00:06:14.248 --> 00:06:17.710
- And when you're
playing basketball,

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:19.462
just, it's a different following.

00:06:19.462 --> 00:06:22.465
I know some of it is
home, but just all

00:06:22.465 --> 00:06:23.758
about support and following.

00:06:23.758 --> 00:06:25.134
There's so many
people that I run

00:06:25.134 --> 00:06:28.471
into that are from Naperville or
from the Chicagoland area that

00:06:28.471 --> 00:06:30.515
have followed me
my entire career.
- Right.

00:06:30.515 --> 00:06:35.103
And to say that that hasn't
been a part of my journey?
- No.

00:06:35.103 --> 00:06:36.521
- It just was only right
to go back home.

00:06:36.521 --> 00:06:39.899
So I do believe in full
circle and things like that.

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:41.943
But I think it was
just the right time, you know?

00:06:41.943 --> 00:06:43.903
To go back to what
I'm used to and go

00:06:43.903 --> 00:06:46.364
back to where I'm from. To end.
Because, you know, Channing,

00:06:46.364 --> 00:06:47.281
the end is near.

00:06:47.281 --> 00:06:47.949
It's coming.

00:06:47.949 --> 00:06:50.076
- Ooh, listen, the end
ain't too bad, girl.

00:06:50.076 --> 00:06:51.452
You could do these
shows with me.

00:06:51.452 --> 00:06:52.370
- It's coming.

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:54.330
I'm ready to do this
more on the regular.

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:57.417
- If I think about all the Chicago
players that have come out,

00:06:57.417 --> 00:07:01.879
No. 1 is a loyalty
to that city and a loyalty

00:07:01.879 --> 00:07:05.591
to pass on, like, the
tradition of griminess.

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:08.761
Every Chicago player is willing
to, No. 1, throw hands,

00:07:08.761 --> 00:07:12.807
and No. 2, elbow you if it
costs winning the game, right?

00:07:12.807 --> 00:07:14.559
- I think it's a little
bit of everything,

00:07:14.559 --> 00:07:19.647
because, you know, I think Illinois as a
whole has a style and a brand

00:07:19.647 --> 00:07:20.398
of basketball.

00:07:20.398 --> 00:07:23.609
For me, personally,
I think I'm fluid.

00:07:23.609 --> 00:07:25.278
I mean, you said throw hands.

00:07:25.278 --> 00:07:26.279
I'll definitely do that.

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:27.613
But at the same time,

00:07:27.613 --> 00:07:29.323
- I've seen you elbow
many a people.

00:07:29.323 --> 00:07:31.826
[Both laughing]

00:07:31.826 --> 00:07:34.120
- Oh, don't tell them about all
those times on set, Channing.

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:38.040
Nah, but the versatility,
I think, is a big part,

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:39.959
just being effective.

00:07:39.959 --> 00:07:42.170
But I don't want
it to get confused.

00:07:42.170 --> 00:07:46.716
Like, we're extremely skilled,
versatile, fluid.
- Yeah, oh, yeah.

00:07:46.716 --> 00:07:48.843
- But I would take
our five any day.

00:07:48.843 --> 00:07:50.261
- Oh, for sure.

00:07:50.261 --> 00:07:53.055
You were one of the first
women Adidas athletes.

00:07:53.055 --> 00:07:55.349
There have not been a lot
of women's signature shoes.

00:07:55.349 --> 00:07:57.393
Do you think that's gonna
start coming around?

00:07:57.393 --> 00:08:01.981
And how can that culture
change where, you know, my daughter can

00:08:01.981 --> 00:08:03.900
get her first Candace Parkers?

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:06.903
- Well, here's the first
and foremost, Channing.

00:08:06.903 --> 00:08:11.616
I've been fortunate enough
to be with partnerships

00:08:11.616 --> 00:08:12.700
for a long time.

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:15.036
It's really important to me
to do something authentic,

00:08:15.036 --> 00:08:18.581
and loyalty is huge
to me, honestly.

00:08:18.581 --> 00:08:20.958
And that's why it
was so difficult

00:08:20.958 --> 00:08:22.793
for me to leave the Sparks.
- Right, right.

00:08:22.793 --> 00:08:24.962
- Because usually when
I start something,

00:08:24.962 --> 00:08:26.464
I've always been
taught to finish.

00:08:26.464 --> 00:08:28.174
And I did.

00:08:28.174 --> 00:08:30.051
My contract was up,
so I was able to go.

00:08:30.051 --> 00:08:31.177
But the same with Adidas.

00:08:31.177 --> 00:08:34.805
I have been three stripes
basically since my junior year

00:08:34.805 --> 00:08:35.848
in high school.

00:08:35.848 --> 00:08:37.099
I've been in Adidas since,

00:08:37.099 --> 00:08:37.600
- Ooh, fire.

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:41.062
- the first Kobes that
came out, the moon boots.

00:08:41.062 --> 00:08:41.938
- Oh, the moon joints.

00:08:41.938 --> 00:08:43.648
- I've been in Adidas that long.

00:08:43.648 --> 00:08:46.108
It's always been a
part of what I'm doing,

00:08:46.108 --> 00:08:49.195
and I've always had a strong
relationship with them.

00:08:49.195 --> 00:08:51.364
So when I got out of college
after being four years

00:08:51.364 --> 00:08:54.367
of Adidas at Tennessee—at the
time, Tennessee was Adidas—

00:08:54.367 --> 00:08:55.326
it was a no-brainer.

00:08:55.326 --> 00:08:57.495
To be honest with you,
it is important for me

00:08:57.495 --> 00:09:00.331
to make a shoe
that's a signature

00:09:00.331 --> 00:09:02.124
and represented by a woman.
- Right.

00:09:02.124 --> 00:09:05.002
But at the same,
I've gone into Adidas

00:09:05.002 --> 00:09:08.965
and carved out this
niche for all genders.

00:09:08.965 --> 00:09:13.553
I want little boys to be
OK with wearing my shoes,

00:09:13.553 --> 00:09:16.013
and so that's been a
huge effort for me.

00:09:16.013 --> 00:09:18.266
- Are they gonna have some
tight colorways or what? Listen.

00:09:18.266 --> 00:09:20.226
- We got you, Channing.

00:09:20.226 --> 00:09:21.561
It's in the works.
- OK.

00:09:21.561 --> 00:09:23.813
And a lot of it was
sizing issues, too,

00:09:23.813 --> 00:09:26.107
like making things
gender-neutral,
- Right. Right, right.

00:09:26.107 --> 00:09:28.568
but making them in men's sizes.

00:09:28.568 --> 00:09:31.153
And there had been no, up until this
point, there had been

00:09:31.153 --> 00:09:32.655
women that had signature shoes.

00:09:32.655 --> 00:09:34.991
You talk about Lisa
Leslie, Sheryl Swoopes.
- Right, right.

00:09:34.991 --> 00:09:36.659
- But there hadn't been
women that had had

00:09:36.659 --> 00:09:38.869
signature shoes in men's sizes.

00:09:38.869 --> 00:09:41.247
And so that was important
to me, was to make more

00:09:41.247 --> 00:09:44.584
of a gender-neutral so that we
introduce the fact that it is

00:09:44.584 --> 00:09:50.548
OK to then dream about, you know, wearing
different shoes and different

00:09:50.548 --> 00:09:51.591
collections, similar to like

00:09:51.591 --> 00:09:52.508
you're seeing Beyoncé.

00:09:52.508 --> 00:09:56.012
You're seeing a lot of men
that are rocking Ivy Park.
- OK.

00:09:56.012 --> 00:09:58.389
- And so I think it's similar
to like that kind of thing,

00:09:58.389 --> 00:09:59.932
where it's OK to
look up to women

00:09:59.932 --> 00:10:01.601
and see women in
leadership positions.

00:10:01.601 --> 00:10:04.729
And I think it starts with shoe
companies and organizations

00:10:04.729 --> 00:10:08.274
and your partnerships putting
women out there like that.
- Right.

00:10:08.274 --> 00:10:11.235
Speaking of that, because you
helped my transitions better

00:10:11.235 --> 00:10:14.780
than I could, we're going
to go to this third wine.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:18.075
So Stag's Leap Wine Cellars
are known for their Napa Cabs.

00:10:18.075 --> 00:10:21.203
That's pretty much all
I drink, are Napa Cabs.

00:10:21.203 --> 00:10:24.040
Gotten into a little
